# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Overview of error mitigation techniques {#overview-of-error-mitigation-techniques}"
msgstr "Pangkalahatang-ideya ng mga teknik ng error mitigation {#overview-of-error-mitigation-techniques}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Error mitigation refers to any process that attempts to reduce the effects of errors in quantum computations after they have occurred. It is different from suppression, which attempts to prevent errors, and from error correction, which encodes information across many physical qubits and checks the consistency in that information to preserve information in a single logical qubit. Throughout this lesson, error mitigation techniques can best be understood as methods for constructing better estimators of ideal expectation values or better samplers of probability distributions, **not** as methods for correcting quantum states during execution. We will examine the error mitigation methods below, all of which are available in Qiskit and its addons, and which are promising tools in the pursuit of quantum advantage.\n"
msgstr "Ang error mitigation ay tumutukoy sa anumang proseso na sumusubok na bawasan ang epekto ng mga error sa mga quantum computation matapos ang mga ito mangyari. Iba ito sa suppression, na sumusubok pigilan ang mga error, at sa error correction, na nag-eencode ng impormasyon sa maraming pisikal na qubit at sinusuri ang consistency ng impormasyong iyon para mapanatili ang impormasyon sa iisang logical qubit. Sa buong araling ito, pinakamainam na maunawaan ang mga teknik ng error mitigation bilang mga pamamaraan para bumuo ng mas mahusay na estimator ng mga ideal na expectation value o mas mahusay na sampler ng mga probability distribution, **hindi** bilang mga pamamaraan para itama ang mga quantum state habang tumatakbo. Susuriin natin sa ibaba ang mga pamamaraan ng error mitigation, na lahat ay available sa Qiskit at sa mga addon nito, at mga promising na tool sa paghahangad ng quantum advantage.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Twirled readout error extinction (TREX)** randomizes the measurement process by applying randomly selected Pauli operations before measurement and then classically undoing their known effects on the observed bitstrings. Under suitable measurement-noise assumptions, this converts the readout error channel into a simpler and more symmetric form that is easier to calibrate and mitigate.\n"
msgstr "Ang **Twirled readout error extinction (TREX)** ay nagra-randomize ng proseso ng pagsukat sa pamamagitan ng paglalapat ng mga random na napiling Pauli operation bago ang pagsukat at pagkatapos ay klasikal na pag-undo sa mga kilalang epekto nito sa mga naobserbahang bitstring. Sa ilalim ng angkop na mga pagpapalagay sa measurement-noise, ginagawa nitong mas simple at mas simetrikong anyo ang readout error channel na mas madaling i-calibrate at i-mitigate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Matrix-free measurement mitigation (M3)** is a scalable measurement-error mitigation technique that avoids explicitly constructing the assignment matrix or its inverse.\n"
msgstr "Ang **Matrix-free measurement mitigation (M3)** ay isang scalable na teknik ng measurement-error mitigation na iniiwasang tahasang buuin ang assignment matrix o ang inverse nito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Zero noise extrapolation (ZNE)** approximates the value an observable would have in the absence of noise by artificially scaling noise by a known factor and extrapolating back to the zero-noise case. This requires that noise scale in a predictable way. That predictability might require other methods discussed in this lesson (pulse stretching, gate folding, and probabilistic error amplification (PEA)).\n"
msgstr "Ang **Zero noise extrapolation (ZNE)** ay tinatantya ang halaga na magkakaroon ng isang observable kung walang noise sa pamamagitan ng artipisyal na pag-scale ng noise sa isang kilalang salik at pag-extrapolate pabalik sa zero-noise na kaso. Nangangailangan ito na ang noise ay mag-scale sa predictable na paraan. Maaaring mangailangan ang predictability na iyon ng iba pang pamamaraang tinalakay sa araling ito (pulse stretching, gate folding, at probabilistic error amplification (PEA)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Probabilistic error cancellation (PEC)** is among the most powerful, but also the most computationally expensive, error-mitigation techniques. It works by learning the noise behavior of the system and attempting to undo the noise by statistically constructing an unbiased estimate of the ideal computation. PEC outputs statistically corrected observables, not error-corrected results.\n"
msgstr "Ang **Probabilistic error cancellation (PEC)** ay isa sa pinakamakapangyarihan, ngunit pinakamahal din sa computation, na teknik ng error mitigation. Gumagana ito sa pamamagitan ng pag-aaral sa noise behavior ng sistema at pagtatangkang i-undo ang noise sa pamamagitan ng istatistikal na pagbuo ng unbiased na tantiya ng ideal na computation. Ang output ng PEC ay mga observable na istatistikal na naitama, hindi mga resultang error-corrected.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will now dive into each of these methods.\n"
msgstr "Susuriin na natin ngayon nang malalim ang bawat isa sa mga pamamaraang ito.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "M3 {#m3}"
msgstr "M3 {#m3}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measurement errors are one of the most persistent and hardware-specific sources of noise in near-term quantum devices. Even when a quantum state is prepared and evolved correctly, the final readout step can misreport the true computational basis outcome. These errors arise from imperfect discrimination between the physical states corresponding to $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$, relaxation during measurement, and imperfections in measurement electronics. As a result, the observed distribution of bitstrings is often a distorted version of the true (ideal) distribution, with probability incorrectly reassigned between outcomes.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga measurement error ay isa sa pinaka-persistent at pinaka-hardware-specific na pinagmumulan ng noise sa mga near-term na quantum device. Kahit tama ang pagkakahanda at pag-evolve ng isang quantum state, maaaring mag-ulat ng maling computational basis outcome ang huling hakbang ng readout. Nagmumula ang mga error na ito sa hindi perpektong pagkilala sa pagitan ng mga pisikal na estadong tumutugma sa $|0\\rangle$ at $|1\\rangle$, relaxation habang sinusukat, at mga imperpeksyon sa measurement electronics. Bilang resulta, ang naobserbahang distribution ng mga bitstring ay madalas na isang baluktot na bersyon ng totoong (ideal) distribution, na may probabilidad na maling nai-reassign sa pagitan ng mga outcome.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Measurement mitigation addresses this problem by explicitly modeling how measurement outcomes are misassigned. In the standard formulation, this behavior is captured by an assignment matrix $A$, whose elements $A_{ij}$ represent the probability of recording outcome $i$ when the true basis state was $j$. If the measurement noise is classical and stationary, the observed probability vector $p_{\\text{obs}}$ is related to the ideal distribution $p_{\\text{ideal}}$ by a simple linear relation,\n"
"$$\n"
"p_{\\text{obs}} = A\\, p_{\\text{ideal}}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Tinutugunan ng measurement mitigation ang problemang ito sa pamamagitan ng tahasang pagmomodelo kung paano nama-misassign ang mga measurement outcome. Sa karaniwang pormulasyon, ang behavior na ito ay kinakatawan ng isang assignment matrix $A$, na ang mga elementong $A_{ij}$ ay kumakatawan sa probabilidad ng pagtatala ng outcome $i$ kapag ang totoong basis state ay $j$. Kung ang measurement noise ay klasikal at stationary, ang naobserbahang probability vector na $p_{\\text{obs}}$ ay may kaugnayan sa ideal na distribution na $p_{\\text{ideal}}$ sa pamamagitan ng simpleng linear na relasyon,\n"
"$$\n"
"p_{\\text{obs}} = A\\, p_{\\text{ideal}}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that the assignment matrix $A$ is a classical stochastic map, not a quantum channel. It acts on classical readouts, not on the underlying quantum states that were sampled to obtain the readouts.\n"
msgstr "Tandaan na ang assignment matrix na $A$ ay isang klasikal na stochastic map, hindi isang quantum channel. Kumikilos ito sa mga klasikal na readout, hindi sa mga pinagbabatayang quantum state na na-sample para makuha ang mga readout.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A histogram of many measurements. Two matrices are also shown. One is a full assignment matrix describing all possible measurements. The other is a reduced matrix functioning as an assignment matrix only in the subspace of measured states.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/m3-counts.avif)\n"
msgstr "![A histogram of many measurements. Two matrices are also shown. One is a full assignment matrix describing all possible measurements. The other is a reduced matrix functioning as an assignment matrix only in the subspace of measured states.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/m3-counts.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Matrix-free measurement mitigation (M3) uses this structure to construct a mitigated estimate of $p_{\\text{ideal}}$ without ever forming or inverting the full matrix $A$, which would be infeasible for large numbers of qubits. Instead, M3 exploits the fact that many near-term quantum workloads produce highly peaked or low-entropy distributions, so that mitigation focuses only on the subset of outcomes that significantly contribute to the observed distribution.\n"
msgstr "Ginagamit ng Matrix-free measurement mitigation (M3) ang istrukturang ito para bumuo ng mitigated na tantiya ng $p_{\\text{ideal}}$ nang hindi kailanman binubuo o ini-invert ang buong matrix na $A$, na hindi magagawa para sa malaking bilang ng qubit. Sa halip, sinasamantala ng M3 ang katotohanang maraming near-term na quantum workload ay gumagawa ng mga distribution na mataas ang peak o mababa ang entropy, kaya ang mitigation ay nakatuon lamang sa subset ng mga outcome na malaki ang ambag sa naobserbahang distribution.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To build the assignment model, additional calibration experiments must be run on the quantum hardware. These typically involve preparing known computational basis states and recording how they are reported by the measurement system. The calibration overhead scales with the number of measured qubits, while the computational cost of mitigation depends on the structure of the observed distribution. This cost is far smaller than what would be required to characterize the full $2^n \\times 2^n$ assignment matrix explicitly. While this calibration overhead is real, it is lightweight compared to circuit-level techniques like zero-noise extrapolation or probabilistic error cancellation, making M3 a practical and widely used tool for mitigating readout errors in near-term quantum experiments.\n"
msgstr "Para buuin ang assignment model, kailangang magpatakbo ng mga karagdagang calibration experiment sa quantum hardware. Karaniwang kasama rito ang paghahanda ng mga kilalang computational basis state at pagtatala kung paano ito iniuulat ng measurement system. Ang calibration overhead ay lumalaki ayon sa bilang ng mga sinukat na qubit, habang ang computational cost ng mitigation ay nakadepende sa istruktura ng naobserbahang distribution. Mas maliit nang malaki ang cost na ito kaysa sa kakailanganin para tahasang i-characterize ang buong $2^n \\times 2^n$ na assignment matrix. Bagama't totoo ang calibration overhead na ito, magaan ito kumpara sa mga circuit-level na teknik tulad ng zero-noise extrapolation o probabilistic error cancellation, kaya ang M3 ay isang praktikal at malawakang ginagamit na tool para sa pag-mitigate ng mga readout error sa mga near-term na quantum experiment.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that calibration is only useful to the extent that the measurement process remains sufficiently stable between the calibration runs and the target experiment.\n"
msgstr "Tandaan na kapaki-pakinabang lamang ang calibration hangga't nananatiling sapat na stable ang proseso ng pagsukat sa pagitan ng mga calibration run at ng target na experiment.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will first carry out a simple case by hand to illustrate how it works, then use tools built into Qiskit to show the use of M3 at a more realistic scale.\n"
msgstr "Magsasagawa muna tayo ng simpleng kaso nang mano-mano para ilarawan kung paano ito gumagana, at pagkatapos ay gagamit ng mga tool na built-in sa Qiskit para ipakita ang paggamit ng M3 sa mas makatotohanang sukat.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "M3 small example {#m3-small-example}"
msgstr "Maliit na halimbawa ng M3 {#m3-small-example}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We start by building a simple Bell state.\n"
msgstr "Magsisimula tayo sa pagbuo ng simpleng Bell state.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can use a simulator with a noise model because we are restricting this example to a very small circuit. We explicitly configure our noise model to have readout error of the form:\n"
msgstr "Maaari tayong gumamit ng simulator na may noise model dahil nililimitahan natin ang halimbawang ito sa napakaliit na circuit. Tahasan nating kino-configure ang noise model natin para magkaroon ng readout error sa anyong:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "`if true=0: [95% chance of reading 0, 5% chance of reading 1]`\n"
msgstr "`if true=0: [95% chance of reading 0, 5% chance of reading 1]`\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "`if true=1: [8% chance of reading 0, 92% chance of reading 1]`\n"
msgstr "`if true=1: [8% chance of reading 0, 92% chance of reading 1]`\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "These readout error rates are much higher than what one sees in real IBM&reg; quantum computers. The high values have been chosen for pedagogical reasons, so that we can clearly see the effect of M3.\n"
msgstr "Mas mataas nang malaki ang mga readout error rate na ito kaysa sa nakikita sa mga totoong IBM&reg; quantum computer. Pinili ang mataas na mga halaga para sa layuning pang-edukasyon, para malinaw nating makita ang epekto ng M3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can run the circuit on the noisy simulator to observe the effects of readout error.\n"
msgstr "Maaari nating patakbuhin ang circuit sa noisy simulator para obserbahan ang mga epekto ng readout error.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In our toy example, **we defined the readout error** for our noise model. But when using a **real quantum computer**, the readout error is not known a priori. Instead, it must be estimated experimentally. For this, we create circuits to prepare a qubit in a known computational basis state $|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$ and measure it. In the absence of readout error we would measure exactly the prepared state. By collecting statistics from these measurements, we can build an assignment matrix describing the readout error. In this toy example, it should look similar to the one we defined.\n"
msgstr "Sa toy example natin, **tayo ang nagtakda ng readout error** para sa noise model natin. Ngunit kapag gumagamit ng **totoong quantum computer**, hindi alam nang maaga ang readout error. Sa halip, kailangan itong tantiyahin sa pamamagitan ng eksperimento. Para dito, gumagawa tayo ng mga circuit para ihanda ang isang qubit sa kilalang computational basis state na $|0\\rangle$ o $|1\\rangle$ at sukatin ito. Kung walang readout error, eksaktong ang inihandang estado ang masusukat natin. Sa pagkolekta ng estadistika mula sa mga pagsukat na ito, makakabuo tayo ng assignment matrix na naglalarawan sa readout error. Sa toy example na ito, dapat itong magmukhang katulad ng ating itinakda.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For a more realistic system, statistics would need to be accumulated for each physical qubit. This is sometimes non-trivial, because transpilation maps an abstract circuit to hardware and the qubit number changes. We will revisit that in the next example. In this simulator example, we assigned the same readout-error model to every qubit, so a single characterization is sufficient.\n"
msgstr "Para sa mas makatotohanang sistema, kailangang tipunin ang estadistika para sa bawat pisikal na qubit. Minsan hindi ito madali, dahil minamapa ng transpilation ang isang abstract na circuit sa hardware at nagbabago ang numero ng qubit. Babalikan natin iyon sa susunod na halimbawa. Sa simulator example na ito, itinalaga natin ang parehong readout-error model sa bawat qubit, kaya sapat na ang isang characterization.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that this is very close to the readout error matrix used to construct the simulator.\n"
msgstr "Tandaan na napakalapit nito sa readout error matrix na ginamit sa pagbuo ng simulator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Again, on a real quantum computer we would have statistics like these for multiple physical qubits. For this simulator, we assume the two qubits have identical and independent readout errors, allowing us to construct the two-qubit assignment matrix `A_2q` as a tensor product of the corresponding one-qubit matrices. Also note that the point of M3 is **not** to construct the entire assignment matrix. Instead, M3 focuses on the subset of outcomes that significantly contribute to the observed distribution. We will build the entire matrix for our very small example to see how it works. The implementation of M3 in `qiskit-ibm-runtime` is more sophisticated than this toy example because it must handle many qubits with distinct readout characteristics while still avoiding construction of the full assignment matrix.\n"
msgstr "Muli, sa totoong quantum computer ay magkakaroon tayo ng ganitong estadistika para sa maraming pisikal na qubit. Para sa simulator na ito, ipinapalagay nating magkapareho at independent ang readout error ng dalawang qubit, na nagpapahintulot sa atin na buuin ang two-qubit assignment matrix na `A_2q` bilang tensor product ng mga katumbas na one-qubit matrix. Tandaan din na ang punto ng M3 ay **hindi** ang buuin ang buong assignment matrix. Sa halip, nakatuon ang M3 sa subset ng mga outcome na malaki ang ambag sa naobserbahang distribution. Bubuuin natin ang buong matrix para sa napakaliit nating halimbawa para makita kung paano ito gumagana. Mas sopistikado ang implementasyon ng M3 sa `qiskit-ibm-runtime` kaysa sa toy example na ito dahil kailangan nitong humawak ng maraming qubit na may magkakaibang readout characteristic habang iniiwasan pa ring buuin ang buong assignment matrix.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For this example we assume that readout errors on different qubits are independent, allowing the two-qubit assignment matrix to be constructed as a tensor product of the corresponding one-qubit matrices.\n"
msgstr "Para sa halimbawang ito, ipinapalagay nating independent ang mga readout error sa magkakaibang qubit, na nagpapahintulot na buuin ang two-qubit assignment matrix bilang tensor product ng mga katumbas na one-qubit matrix.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We obtain the raw and mitigated results for comparison.\n"
msgstr "Kukunin natin ang raw at mitigated na mga resulta para sa paghahambing.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "At this point, we have experimentally characterized how the measurement process distorts ideal outcome probabilities. Our goal is now to undo this distortion and apply the resulting correction to the raw probabilities.\n"
msgstr "Sa puntong ito, na-characterize na natin sa pamamagitan ng eksperimento kung paano binabaluktot ng proseso ng pagsukat ang mga ideal na probabilidad ng outcome. Layunin natin ngayong i-undo ang pagbaluktot na ito at ilapat ang nagresultang correction sa mga raw na probabilidad.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Because we are performing a linear inversion rather than a physical correction, the resulting \"probabilities\" need not always be positive. Very small negative quasi-probabilities are expected and reflect the fact that measurement mitigation produces an estimator rather than a physical probability distribution.\n"
msgstr "Dahil nagsasagawa tayo ng linear inversion sa halip na pisikal na correction, hindi laging positibo ang mga nagreresultang \"probabilidad\". Inaasahan ang napakaliliit na negatibong quasi-probability at sinasalamin nito ang katotohanang ang measurement mitigation ay gumagawa ng estimator sa halip na pisikal na probability distribution.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We convert these to counts for plotting.\n"
msgstr "Kino-convert natin ang mga ito sa counts para sa pag-plot.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally we look at the raw, mitigated, and ideal distributions. The mitigated \"counts\" below are only a visualization aid obtained by rescaling the mitigated quasi-probabilities.\n"
msgstr "Sa wakas, tinitingnan natin ang raw, mitigated, at ideal na mga distribution. Ang mga mitigated na \"counts\" sa ibaba ay pantulong lamang sa visualization na nakuha sa pamamagitan ng pag-rescale ng mga mitigated na quasi-probability.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/f10a0dda-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/f10a0dda-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Clearly, the M3-mitigated results are much closer to the ideal distributions, and we can see why.\n"
msgstr "Malinaw na mas malapit sa ideal na mga distribution ang mga resultang M3-mitigated, at makikita natin kung bakit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Applying M3 to more realistic workflows is much more complex. Consider:\n"
"* Real quantum computers contain many physical qubits, each with its own readout-error characteristics. These errors must be modeled and combined appropriately.\n"
"* The full assignment matrix $A$ grows exponentially with the number of qubits and quickly becomes impractical to construct explicitly. This is why M3 focuses on the subset of outcomes that contribute significantly to the observed distribution rather than constructing the entire assignment matrix.\n"
"* For real problems of interest, the ideal distribution might not be known.\n"
msgstr ""
"Mas komplikado nang malaki ang paglalapat ng M3 sa mas makatotohanang mga workflow. Isaalang-alang:\n"
"* Ang mga totoong quantum computer ay may maraming pisikal na qubit, bawat isa ay may sariling readout-error characteristic. Kailangang imodelo at pagsamahin nang naaangkop ang mga error na ito.\n"
"* Ang buong assignment matrix na $A$ ay lumalaki nang exponential ayon sa bilang ng qubit at mabilis na nagiging imposibleng buuin nang tahasan. Kaya nakatuon ang M3 sa subset ng mga outcome na malaki ang ambag sa naobserbahang distribution sa halip na buuin ang buong assignment matrix.\n"
"* Para sa mga totoong problemang interesado tayo, maaaring hindi alam ang ideal na distribution.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For all of these reasons, the full mitigation workflow is encapsulated in the `mthree` Qiskit addon.\n"
msgstr "Dahil sa lahat ng mga dahilang ito, nakapaloob ang buong mitigation workflow sa `mthree` Qiskit addon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Larger M3 example {#larger-m3-example}"
msgstr "Mas malaking halimbawa ng M3 {#larger-m3-example}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We now apply M3 to a larger circuit, one for which determining the relevant subspace of $A$ by hand would be cumbersome, but which is still simple enough that we know the ideal distribution: a GHZ state. We start importing the `mthree` package and defining a GHZ quantum circuit.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon ay ilalapat natin ang M3 sa mas malaking circuit, isa kung saan magiging nakakapagod ang mano-manong pagtukoy sa kaugnay na subspace ng $A$, ngunit sapat pa ring simple para malaman natin ang ideal na distribution: isang GHZ state. Magsisimula tayo sa pag-import ng package na `mthree` at pagdedefine ng isang GHZ quantum circuit.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d7e4b190-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d7e4b190-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To run this on a real quantum computer, we import `qiskit_ibm_runtime` and other necessary packages. We use `SamplerV2` to obtain a distribution of outputs.\n"
msgstr "Para patakbuhin ito sa totoong quantum computer, ini-import natin ang `qiskit_ibm_runtime` at iba pang kinakailangang package. Ginagamit natin ang `SamplerV2` para makakuha ng distribution ng mga output.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we transpile the circuit to our backend. We are setting the optimization level to 0 here in order to focus on the effects of M3, without adding in additional tools. This means the final fidelity will not be representative of what is achievable on IBM Quantum&reg; hardware using the full suite of available tools, but it allows us to focus specifically on the usefulness of M3.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon ay tina-transpile natin ang circuit sa ating backend. Itinatakda natin dito ang optimization level sa 0 para tumuon sa mga epekto ng M3, nang walang dagdag na mga tool. Nangangahulugan ito na hindi kakatawan ang huling fidelity sa kayang makamit sa IBM Quantum&reg; hardware gamit ang buong suite ng mga available na tool, ngunit pinapayagan tayo nitong tumuon partikular sa pagiging kapaki-pakinabang ng M3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We now configure the Sampler, again disabling additional error-suppression techniques such as dynamical decoupling and twirling.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon ay kino-configure natin ang Sampler, muling dini-disable ang mga karagdagang teknik ng error suppression tulad ng dynamical decoupling at twirling.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we execute our job.\n"
msgstr "Sa wakas, isasagawa natin ang ating job.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can extract the raw counts from the circuit above.\n"
msgstr "Maaari nating kunin ang mga raw count mula sa circuit sa itaas.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we run `mthree` on our backend to characterize the assignment model used for measurement mitigation. We rescale the raw distribution using that information. Note that this can sometimes result in very small negative probabilities (hence the term **quasi-probabilities**).\n"
msgstr "Ngayon ay pinapatakbo natin ang `mthree` sa ating backend para i-characterize ang assignment model na ginagamit sa measurement mitigation. Nire-rescale natin ang raw distribution gamit ang impormasyong iyon. Tandaan na minsan ay maaari itong magresulta sa napakaliliit na negatibong probabilidad (kaya ang terminong **quasi-probabilities**).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `mthree` correction produces quasi-probabilities, rather than counts. We can rescale to counts by hand. The helper function below converts to counts and rounds to integer values. Also, for large circuits, there will be many erroneous strings with very few counts, but which clutter a histogram. The second helper function below trims away any strings with fewer counts than some threshold we choose, to give us a clear view of the dominant terms in the distribution.\n"
msgstr "Ang correction ng `mthree` ay gumagawa ng mga quasi-probability, sa halip na mga count. Maaari nating i-rescale sa mga count nang mano-mano. Kino-convert ng helper function sa ibaba sa mga count at nire-round sa mga integer na halaga. Gayundin, para sa malalaking circuit, magkakaroon ng maraming maling string na may napakakaunting count, ngunit nagpapagulo sa histogram. Tinatanggal ng pangalawang helper function sa ibaba ang anumang string na may mas kaunting count kaysa sa threshold na pipiliin natin, para magbigay ng malinaw na tanaw sa mga nangingibabaw na termino sa distribution.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can also easily produce the theoretical, ideal distribution in which we have equal probabilities for the all-zero and all-one states, and zero probability for all other outcomes, for comparison.\n"
msgstr "Madali rin tayong makakagawa ng teoretikal, ideal na distribution kung saan pantay ang probabilidad ng all-zero at all-one na mga estado, at zero ang probabilidad ng lahat ng iba pang outcome, para sa paghahambing.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we can visualize our results.\n"
msgstr "Sa wakas, maaari nating i-visualize ang ating mga resulta.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/4a451fe6-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/4a451fe6-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The mitigated results are closer to the ideal distribution than the unmitigated results. We can quantify this better using counts for the ideal bitstrings.\n"
msgstr "Mas malapit sa ideal na distribution ang mga mitigated na resulta kaysa sa mga unmitigated. Mas maisusukat natin ito gamit ang mga count para sa mga ideal na bitstring.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This simple tool improved our fidelity by 26% relative to the unmitigated fidelity, with fairly low overhead. Clearly M3 can be very valuable. But the GHZ circuit was selected as a good use case, because it is highly peaked on two output states. Some problems result in more evenly-distributed counts (high entropy states). For very deep circuits with many qubits, gate errors and noise unrelated to measurement errors will often result in a high-entropy state. In such cases, M3 operates on a state space that is quite large. There are three possible approaches to this:\n"
msgstr "Pinahusay ng simpleng tool na ito ang ating fidelity ng 26% kumpara sa unmitigated na fidelity, na may medyo mababang overhead. Malinaw na maaaring maging napakahalaga ng M3. Ngunit pinili ang GHZ circuit bilang magandang use case, dahil mataas ang peak nito sa dalawang output state. Ang ilang problema ay nagreresulta sa mas pantay na distribusyon ng mga count (mga high-entropy state). Para sa napakalalalim na circuit na maraming qubit, madalas na magreresulta sa high-entropy state ang mga gate error at noise na walang kinalaman sa measurement error. Sa ganitong mga kaso, gumagana ang M3 sa isang state space na medyo malaki. May tatlong posibleng paraan dito:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '(1) '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Accept that this is a limitation of M3.\n"
msgstr "Tanggapin na ito ay limitasyon ng M3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '(2) '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that the upper bound on the space of the matrix formed by M3 is `num_shots` $\\times$ `num_shots`, rather than $2^N \\times 2^N$, with the former being much smaller for large number of qubits $N$.\n"
msgstr "Tandaan na ang upper bound sa espasyo ng matrix na binubuo ng M3 ay `num_shots` $\\times$ `num_shots`, sa halip na $2^N \\times 2^N$, kung saan mas maliit nang malaki ang una para sa malaking bilang ng qubit na $N$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '(3) '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Only with great caution, remove very low-probability bitstrings from the raw counts fed into M3. This might introduce bias, and the limitation of the support of your states can interfere with convergence in approaches like the Krylov method. This should only be done when you know exactly why you are doing it and can anticipate all its effects. That is why such trimming is not a built-in feature of M3.\n"
msgstr "Nang may matinding pag-iingat lamang, alisin ang mga bitstring na napakababa ng probabilidad mula sa mga raw count na ipinapasok sa M3. Maaari itong magdulot ng bias, at ang paglilimita sa support ng iyong mga estado ay maaaring makagambala sa convergence sa mga pamamaraang tulad ng Krylov method. Dapat lamang itong gawin kapag alam mong eksakto kung bakit mo ito ginagawa at mahuhulaan mo ang lahat ng epekto nito. Kaya hindi built-in na feature ng M3 ang ganitong pag-trim.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For more on the `mthree` Qiskit addon see examples, installation instructions, background, and API references in its [documentation](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-mthree/).\n"
msgstr "Para sa higit pa tungkol sa `mthree` Qiskit addon, tingnan ang mga halimbawa, tagubilin sa pag-install, background, at API reference sa [dokumentasyon](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-mthree/) nito.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Twirled readout error extinction (TREX) {#twirled-readout-error-extinction-trex}"
msgstr "Twirled readout error extinction (TREX) {#twirled-readout-error-extinction-trex}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Twirled Readout Error Extinction (TREX) is a measurement-error mitigation technique used on modern quantum computers. Its goal is to remove bias introduced by classical readout errors without requiring full characterization or inversion of the measurement assignment matrix.\n"
msgstr "Ang Twirled Readout Error Extinction (TREX) ay isang teknik ng measurement-error mitigation na ginagamit sa mga makabagong quantum computer. Layunin nitong alisin ang bias na dulot ng mga klasikal na readout error nang hindi nangangailangan ng buong characterization o inversion ng measurement assignment matrix.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "TREX is not error correction and does not improve state fidelity. Instead, it ensures that expectation value estimators are unbiased with respect to readout noise, up to statistical error, by averaging over randomized measurement bases and rescaling. On IBM Quantum hardware, TREX is the default mitigation method for expectation-value estimation and corresponds to `resilience_level = 1` in the IBM Quantum Estimator primitive.\n"
msgstr "Ang TREX ay hindi error correction at hindi nagpapabuti ng state fidelity. Sa halip, tinitiyak nitong unbiased ang mga expectation value estimator kaugnay ng readout noise, hanggang sa statistical error, sa pamamagitan ng pag-average sa mga randomized na measurement basis at pag-rescale. Sa IBM Quantum hardware, ang TREX ang default na mitigation method para sa expectation-value estimation at tumutugma sa `resilience_level = 1` sa IBM Quantum Estimator primitive.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nature of readout errors on superconducting hardware {#nature-of-readout-errors-on-superconducting-hardware}"
msgstr "Kalikasan ng mga readout error sa superconducting hardware {#nature-of-readout-errors-on-superconducting-hardware}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "On IBM superconducting qubits, readout errors are dominated by:\n"
msgstr "Sa mga IBM superconducting qubit, nangingibabaw sa readout error ang:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Asymmetric bit-flip probabilities (for example, $|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$ more frequently than the reverse)\n"
msgstr "Mga asymmetric na bit-flip probability (halimbawa, $|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$ nang mas madalas kaysa sa kabaligtaran)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "State-dependent relaxation during measurement itself\n"
msgstr "State-dependent na relaxation sa mismong pagsukat\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mild but non-negligible crosstalk between neighboring readout resonators\n"
msgstr "Banayad ngunit hindi mapapabayaang crosstalk sa pagitan ng magkakalapit na readout resonator\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Importantly, these errors can often be modeled as a classical stochastic process acting on measurement outcomes. TREX exploits a standard idea from noise tailoring: randomization (twirling) of the measurement procedure. TREX typically proceeds in two stages:\n"
msgstr "Mahalaga, ang mga error na ito ay madalas na mamodelo bilang klasikal na stochastic process na kumikilos sa mga measurement outcome. Sinasamantala ng TREX ang isang karaniwang ideya mula sa noise tailoring: ang randomization (twirling) ng measurement procedure. Karaniwang dumadaan ang TREX sa dalawang yugto:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Twirling and classical \"untwirling\"\n"
msgstr "Twirling at klasikal na \"untwirling\"\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rescaling of expectation values using calibration circuits\n"
msgstr "Pag-rescale ng mga expectation value gamit ang mga calibration circuit\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose an expectation value of observable $O$ is related to noisy estimates, like the following:\n"
msgstr "Ipagpalagay na ang expectation value ng observable na $O$ ay may kaugnayan sa mga noisy na tantiya, tulad ng sumusunod:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{meas}+b\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{meas}+b\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where $a$ is a fixed factor that scales the estimate and $b$ is a bias from sources described above. We are using $\\approx$ here as a reminder that we are only addressing readout error here; there is still gate error to consider.\n"
msgstr "kung saan ang $a$ ay isang nakapirming salik na nag-scale sa tantiya at ang $b$ ay isang bias mula sa mga pinagmumulang inilarawan sa itaas. Ginagamit natin ang $\\approx$ dito bilang paalala na readout error lamang ang tinutugunan natin dito; may gate error pa ring dapat isaalang-alang.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Just before measurement, each qubit is randomly conjugated with a Pauli operator (like I or X). Measurement outcomes are classically untwirled afterward.\n"
msgstr "Bago lamang ang pagsukat, bawat qubit ay random na kino-conjugate gamit ang isang Pauli operator (tulad ng I o X). Pagkatapos ay klasikal na ina-untwirl ang mga measurement outcome.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A diagram showing how Pauli gates are used in measurement twirling.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/trex-diagram.avif)\n"
msgstr "![A diagram showing how Pauli gates are used in measurement twirling.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/trex-diagram.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Averaging over these randomizations symmetrizes the readout channel. As a result, systematic asymmetries in the readout process are converted into a more symmetric error channel whose remaining effect can be treated through calibration and rescaling. In simple terms, if for example there was a bias toward the $|0\\rangle$ state, we would be missing some measurements of $|1\\rangle$. By twirling around the measurement, we randomly interchange which logical outcome is associated with the physical readout process, then undo that interchange classically afterward. Averaging over many such randomized measurements removes the systematic preference for one reported outcome over the other. This removes the dominant measurement bias under the assumptions of the TREX model, so that we have:\n"
msgstr "Ang pag-average sa mga randomization na ito ay nagsisimetriko sa readout channel. Bilang resulta, ang mga sistematikong asymmetry sa proseso ng readout ay ginagawang mas simetrikong error channel na ang natitirang epekto ay maaaring tugunan sa pamamagitan ng calibration at rescaling. Sa simpleng salita, kung halimbawa ay may bias patungo sa $|0\\rangle$ na estado, mawawalan tayo ng ilang pagsukat ng $|1\\rangle$. Sa pag-twirl sa paligid ng pagsukat, random nating pinagpapalit kung aling logical outcome ang iniuugnay sa pisikal na proseso ng readout, at pagkatapos ay klasikal na ina-undo ang palitang iyon. Ang pag-average sa maraming ganitong randomized na pagsukat ay nag-aalis ng sistematikong pagkiling sa isang iniulat na outcome kaysa sa isa pa. Inaalis nito ang nangingibabaw na measurement bias sa ilalim ng mga pagpapalagay ng TREX model, kaya mayroon tayong:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{twirled}+0\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{twirled}+0\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "However, expectation values might still need to be rescaled. The second stage is using calibration circuits (circuits with known outcomes) to estimate the scaling factor $a$. This can then be used to obtain a greatly enhanced estimate.\n"
msgstr "Gayunpaman, maaaring kailanganin pa ring i-rescale ang mga expectation value. Ang ikalawang yugto ay ang paggamit ng mga calibration circuit (mga circuit na may kilalang outcome) para tantiyahin ang scaling factor na $a$. Magagamit ito para makakuha ng lubhang pinahusay na tantiya.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{TREX} = a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{twirled}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{TREX} = a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{twirled}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Expectation values of Pauli observables become unbiased estimators of their ideal values. No explicit noise model is learned or inverted, which is why TREX is numerically stable and inexpensive. Again, gate error has not been addressed here.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga expectation value ng mga Pauli observable ay nagiging unbiased na estimator ng kanilang mga ideal na halaga. Walang tahasang noise model na pinag-aaralan o ini-invert, kaya numerically stable at mura ang TREX. Muli, hindi pa natutugunan dito ang gate error.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the estimation of an expectation value $\\langle O\\rangle$ of observable $O$, there can be variance due to finite sampling, there can be many types of incoherent and coherent errors, and there can also be a bias, $\\delta$, due to readout error. This bias arises from sources like the asymmetric transitions we've discussed. In practice, this bias is typically well-modeled as a classical stochastic channel acting after the quantum evolution. TREX transforms the effective measurement channel so that the estimator of $\\langle O\\rangle$ is unbiased with respect to readout error. This is relatively easy to see, using the simplest example of twirling gates: X. If there is asymmetry in $|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$ compared to $|0\\rangle \\rightarrow |1\\rangle$, but we randomly switch which state is being read (flipping back later, classically), then this obviously removes the systematic asymmetry (though finite sampling still means there might be slight stochastic asymmetry in measurement). In practice, twirling gates for TREX are not limited to I and X, but this suffices for an explanation.\n"
msgstr "Sa pagtantiya ng expectation value na $\\langle O\\rangle$ ng observable na $O$, maaaring may variance dahil sa finite sampling, maaaring may maraming uri ng incoherent at coherent na error, at maaari ring may bias, $\\delta$, dahil sa readout error. Nagmumula ang bias na ito sa mga pinagmumulang tulad ng mga asymmetric na transition na tinalakay natin. Sa praktika, karaniwang mahusay na namomodelo ang bias na ito bilang klasikal na stochastic channel na kumikilos pagkatapos ng quantum evolution. Binabago ng TREX ang epektibong measurement channel para ang estimator ng $\\langle O\\rangle$ ay unbiased kaugnay ng readout error. Medyo madali itong makita gamit ang pinakasimpleng halimbawa ng mga twirling gate: X. Kung may asymmetry sa $|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$ kumpara sa $|0\\rangle \\rightarrow |1\\rangle$, ngunit random nating pinapalitan kung aling estado ang binabasa (at ibinabalik sa klasikal na paraan pagkatapos), halatang inaalis nito ang sistematikong asymmetry (bagama't ang finite sampling ay nangangahulugan pa ring maaaring may bahagyang stochastic asymmetry sa pagsukat). Sa praktika, ang mga twirling gate para sa TREX ay hindi limitado sa I at X, ngunit sapat na ito para sa paliwanag.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "TREX allows us to obtain an estimate of an expectation value that is free of bias due to readout error. There could still be a bias from some other source (like coherent over-rotation); TREX targets only readout. Because TREX replaces systematic bias with randomized symmetrization, it generally converts bias into additional stochastic variance, increasing the variance of the estimator at fixed sampling cost.\n"
msgstr "Pinapayagan tayo ng TREX na makakuha ng tantiya ng expectation value na malaya sa bias dahil sa readout error. Maaari pa ring may bias mula sa ibang pinagmulan (tulad ng coherent over-rotation); readout lamang ang tinatarget ng TREX. Dahil pinapalitan ng TREX ang sistematikong bias ng randomized na symmetrization, karaniwan nitong kino-convert ang bias sa karagdagang stochastic variance, na nagpapataas ng variance ng estimator sa nakapirming sampling cost.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr "Suriin ang iyong pag-unawa {#check-your-understanding}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose we use estimator on a circuit that has prepared the state $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ in the estimation of $\\langle Z\\rangle$. Suppose further that measurement errors cause outcomes corresponding to $|1\\rangle$ to be reported as $0$ 10% of the time, while outcomes corresponding to $|0\\rangle$ are reported as $1$ only 1% of the time. The asymmetry between these reporting errors introduces a measurement bias.\n"
msgstr "Ipagpalagay na gumagamit tayo ng estimator sa isang circuit na naghanda ng estadong $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ sa pagtantiya ng $\\langle Z\\rangle$. Ipagpalagay pa na ang mga measurement error ay nagiging sanhi para ang mga outcome na tumutugma sa $|1\\rangle$ ay maiulat bilang $0$ sa 10% ng pagkakataon, habang ang mga outcome na tumutugma sa $|0\\rangle$ ay naiuulat bilang $1$ sa 1% lamang ng pagkakataon. Ang asymmetry sa pagitan ng mga reporting error na ito ay nagdudulot ng measurement bias.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(a) In the absence of any such measurement errors, what would be the ideal expectation value of the observable $\\langle Z \\rangle$?\n"
msgstr "(a) Kung walang ganitong mga measurement error, ano ang magiging ideal na expectation value ng observable na $\\langle Z \\rangle$?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(b) With the asymmetric measurement or readout errors, what is the expectation value of the observable $\\langle Z \\rangle$?\n"
msgstr "(b) Sa mga asymmetric na measurement o readout error, ano ang expectation value ng observable na $\\langle Z \\rangle$?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(c) Consider a limited set of Pauli twirling gates: I and X. Assume I and X are applied randomly with 50% probability in multiple shots. The same measurement error rates still apply. Then the Pauli gate (I or X) is undone classically. Make a table of all possible outcomes and their probabilities due to initial superposition, asymmetric measurement errors, and randomly assigned Pauli gates.\n"
msgstr "(c) Isaalang-alang ang limitadong hanay ng mga Pauli twirling gate: I at X. Ipagpalagay na ang I at X ay random na inilalapat na may 50% probabilidad sa maraming shot. Nalalapat pa rin ang parehong mga measurement error rate. Pagkatapos ay klasikal na ina-undo ang Pauli gate (I o X). Gumawa ng talahanayan ng lahat ng posibleng outcome at ng kanilang mga probabilidad dahil sa paunang superposition, mga asymmetric na measurement error, at random na itinalagang mga Pauli gate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(d) Use the table to determine the expectation value $\\langle Z \\rangle$ using the twirling strategy.\n"
msgstr "(d) Gamitin ang talahanayan para tukuyin ang expectation value na $\\langle Z \\rangle$ gamit ang twirling strategy.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(e) Compare the expectation values: ideal, without twirling, and with twirling.\n"
msgstr "(e) Ihambing ang mga expectation value: ideal, walang twirling, at may twirling.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(f) What has been omitted from this treatment that is found in a full TREX application?\n"
msgstr "(f) Ano ang nalaktawan sa pagtalakay na ito na matatagpuan sa isang buong aplikasyon ng TREX?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Sagot\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(a) You can simply use intuition and symmetry to say $\\langle Z \\rangle = 0$. To show explicitly:\n"
msgstr "(a) Maaari mo lamang gamitin ang intuwisyon at symmetry para sabihing $\\langle Z \\rangle = 0$. Para ipakita nang tahasan:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\langle0|+\\langle1|) Z \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(\\langle0|Z|0\\rangle+\\langle0|Z|1\\rangle+\\langle1|Z|0\\rangle+\\langle1|Z|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}((1)\\langle0|0\\rangle+(-1)\\langle0|1\\rangle+(1)\\langle1|0\\rangle+(-1)\\langle1|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(1+0+0+-1)\\\\\n"
"& = 0\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\langle0|+\\langle1|) Z \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(\\langle0|Z|0\\rangle+\\langle0|Z|1\\rangle+\\langle1|Z|0\\rangle+\\langle1|Z|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}((1)\\langle0|0\\rangle+(-1)\\langle0|1\\rangle+(1)\\langle1|0\\rangle+(-1)\\langle1|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(1+0+0+-1)\\\\\n"
"& = 0\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(b) The state could collapse to either $|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$ when measured, each with 50% chance. But of the 50% that collapse to the $|0\\rangle$ state, 1% of those (or 0.5% of all cases) will be mis-reported as $|1\\rangle$. Of the 50% that collapse to $|1\\rangle$, 10% (or 5% of all cases) will be mis-reported as $|0\\rangle$. This leaves us with:\n"
"* $|0\\rangle$ 54.5%\n"
"* $|1\\rangle$ 45.5%\n"
"Ignoring all other types of possible errors, we would obtain an estimate of\n"
msgstr ""
"(b) Maaaring mag-collapse ang estado sa alinman sa $|0\\rangle$ o $|1\\rangle$ kapag sinukat, bawat isa ay may 50% tsansa. Ngunit sa 50% na nag-collapse sa estadong $|0\\rangle$, 1% nito (o 0.5% ng lahat ng kaso) ay maling maiuulat bilang $|1\\rangle$. Sa 50% na nag-collapse sa $|1\\rangle$, 10% (o 5% ng lahat ng kaso) ay maling maiuulat bilang $|0\\rangle$. Iniiwan tayo nito sa:\n"
"* $|0\\rangle$ 54.5%\n"
"* $|1\\rangle$ 45.5%\n"
"Sa pagbalewala sa lahat ng iba pang uri ng posibleng error, makakakuha tayo ng tantiya na\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle = (0.545)(1)+(0.455)(-1) = 0.09\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle = (0.545)(1)+(0.455)(-1) = 0.09\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(c)\n"
msgstr "(c)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Pauli | Ideal measurement outcome | Outcome after Pauli | Readout error | Undo Pauli | Final reported outcome | Probability |\n"
"|--------------|---------|----------|-------------|------------|------------|------------|\n"
"| I | 0 | 0 | None | I | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 99% = 24.75\\% |\n"
"| I | 0 | 0 | $0 \\rightarrow 1$ | I | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 1% = 0.25\\% |\n"
"| I | 1 | 1 | None | I | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 90% = 22.5\\% |\n"
"| I | 1 | 1 | $1 \\rightarrow 0$ | I | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 10% = 2.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 0 | 1 | None | X | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 90% = 22.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 0 | 1 | $1 \\rightarrow 0$ | X | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 10% = 2.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 1 | 0 | None | X | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 99% = 24.75\\% |\n"
"| X | 1 | 0 | $0 \\rightarrow 1$ | X | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 1% = 0.25\\% |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Pauli | Ideal na measurement outcome | Outcome pagkatapos ng Pauli | Readout error | I-undo ang Pauli | Pangwakas na iniulat na outcome | Probabilidad |\n"
"|--------------|---------|----------|-------------|------------|------------|------------|\n"
"| I | 0 | 0 | Wala | I | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 99% = 24.75\\% |\n"
"| I | 0 | 0 | $0 \\rightarrow 1$ | I | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 1% = 0.25\\% |\n"
"| I | 1 | 1 | Wala | I | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 90% = 22.5\\% |\n"
"| I | 1 | 1 | $1 \\rightarrow 0$ | I | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 10% = 2.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 0 | 1 | Wala | X | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 90% = 22.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 0 | 1 | $1 \\rightarrow 0$ | X | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 10% = 2.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 1 | 0 | Wala | X | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 99% = 24.75\\% |\n"
"| X | 1 | 0 | $0 \\rightarrow 1$ | X | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 1% = 0.25\\% |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(d) From previous parts, we know the expectation values of Z in each of the final states. We need only take the weighted average:\n"
msgstr "(d) Mula sa mga naunang bahagi, alam natin ang mga expectation value ng Z sa bawat huling estado. Kailangan na lamang nating kunin ang weighted average:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = (0.2475)(1)+(0.0025)(-1)+(0.225)(-1)+(0.025)(1)\\\\\n"
"& + (0.225)(1)+(0.025)(-1)+(0.2475)(-1)+(0.0025)(1)\\\\\n"
"& = 0\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = (0.2475)(1)+(0.0025)(-1)+(0.225)(-1)+(0.025)(1)\\\\\n"
"& + (0.225)(1)+(0.025)(-1)+(0.2475)(-1)+(0.0025)(1)\\\\\n"
"& = 0\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(e) In this very simple case our application of twirling recovered exactly the ideal expectation value. That is not typical. What is typical is that the twirling undoes biases like those introduced through asymmetric transitions, and returns an expectation value closer to the ideal.\n"
msgstr "(e) Sa napakasimpleng kasong ito, eksaktong naibalik ng paglalapat natin ng twirling ang ideal na expectation value. Hindi iyon karaniwan. Ang karaniwan ay inaalis ng twirling ang mga bias tulad ng mga ipinakilala ng asymmetric na transition, at nagbabalik ng expectation value na mas malapit sa ideal.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(f) This is a toy model application. There could be several things missing. Notably, we have not run calibration circuits here to estimate a rescaling factor $a$. This is typically necessary.\n"
msgstr "(f) Isa itong toy model na aplikasyon. Maaaring may ilang bagay na kulang. Kapansin-pansin, hindi tayo nagpatakbo rito ng mga calibration circuit para tantiyahin ang rescaling factor na $a$. Karaniwang kinakailangan ito.\n"

