msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Implementation details {#implementation-details}"
msgstr "Mga detalye ng implementasyon {#implementation-details}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "TREX is automatically applied when using the EstimatorV2 primitive with `resilience_level = 1` (the default). Note that it is **not** applied automatically to the Sampler primitive, because Sampler returns full probability distributions rather than expectation values.\n"
msgstr "Awtomatikong inilalapat ang TREX kapag ginagamit ang EstimatorV2 primitive na may `resilience_level = 1` (ang default). Tandaan na **hindi** ito awtomatikong inilalapat sa Sampler primitive, dahil ang Sampler ay nagbabalik ng buong probability distribution sa halip na mga expectation value.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "When TREX is enabled, the runtime generates measurement-twirled circuit variants by inserting random single-qubit Paulis immediately before measurement. Classical post-processing reverses these Paulis. These circuit variants are random across different shots and also across different qubits within a shot. All circuit variants are then combined into a single estimator.\n"
msgstr "Kapag naka-enable ang TREX, gumagawa ang runtime ng mga measurement-twirled na variant ng circuit sa pamamagitan ng pagpasok ng mga random na single-qubit Pauli bago mismo ang pagsukat. Binabaligtad ng klasikal na post-processing ang mga Pauli na ito. Random ang mga variant ng circuit na ito sa iba't ibang shot at gayundin sa iba't ibang qubit sa loob ng isang shot. Pagkatapos ay pinagsasama ang lahat ng variant ng circuit sa iisang estimator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"TREX is very scalable. It requires additional calibration circuits, such that the sampling overhead is typically a small constant factor (often 2-4x).\n"
"But the mitigation is observable-specific, not distribution-preserving.\n"
msgstr ""
"Napaka-scalable ng TREX. Nangangailangan ito ng mga karagdagang calibration circuit, kaya ang sampling overhead ay karaniwang maliit na constant factor (madalas 2-4x).\n"
"Ngunit ang mitigation ay observable-specific, hindi distribution-preserving.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "TREX is designed to integrate cleanly with many error suppression and error mitigation methods, including the following:\n"
msgstr "Dinisenyo ang TREX para madaling isama sa maraming pamamaraan ng error suppression at error mitigation, kabilang ang mga sumusunod:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dynamical decoupling (DD) - acts during idle times, orthogonal to readout mitigation.\n"
msgstr "Dynamical decoupling (DD) - kumikilos sa idle time, orthogonal sa readout mitigation.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Gate or Pauli twirling - often complementary; TREX already relies on twirling but targets only readout noise.\n"
msgstr "Gate o Pauli twirling - madalas na komplementaryo; umaasa na ang TREX sa twirling ngunit readout noise lamang ang tinatarget.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Zero-Noise Extrapolation (ZNE) - ZNE targets gate noise, while TREX handles measurement bias. This combination is used at resilience level 2.\n"
msgstr "Zero-Noise Extrapolation (ZNE) - tinatarget ng ZNE ang gate noise, habang ang TREX ang humahawak sa measurement bias. Ginagamit ang kombinasyong ito sa resilience level 2.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Probabilistic error amplification and probabilistic error cancellation in Estimator workflows.\n"
msgstr "Probabilistic error amplification at probabilistic error cancellation sa mga workflow ng Estimator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "However, some methods cannot (or should not) be combined with TREX:\n"
msgstr "Gayunpaman, may ilang pamamaraang hindi maaari (o hindi dapat) isama sa TREX:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sampler-level probability correction: TREX does not return corrected bitstring probabilities.\n"
msgstr "Sampler-level na probability correction: hindi nagbabalik ang TREX ng mga naitamang bitstring probability.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Post-hoc mitigation on already-mitigated TREX data: double correction can reintroduce bias.\n"
msgstr "Post-hoc na mitigation sa data ng TREX na na-mitigate na: ang double correction ay maaaring magbalik ng bias.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Combining TREX with assignment-matrix-based readout mitigation like M3 is tricky. M3 assumes a fixed measurement channel. TREX deliberately randomizes measurement, invalidating assumption used by M3. In that sense, you cannot simply combine these techniques. However, if you apply measurement twirling during the calibration used in M3, you might be able to leverage both an assignment matrix-like approach and a twirling mitigation approach simultaneously (though this twirling approach is not quite the same as TREX). This is a recurring theme in combining mitigation and suppression techniques: blindly adding techniques fails; adding them carefully such that they are \"aware\" of each other can work and can be highly beneficial.\n"
msgstr "Medyo tricky ang pagsasama ng TREX at ng assignment-matrix-based readout mitigation tulad ng M3. Ipinapalagay ng M3 na fixed ang measurement channel. Sinasadya ng TREX na i-randomize ang measurement, kaya nawawalan ng bisa ang assumption na ginagamit ng M3. Sa ganitong paraan, hindi basta-basta maisasama ang dalawang technique na ito. Gayunpaman, kung maglalapat ka ng measurement twirling habang nasa calibration na ginagamit sa M3, maaari mong magamit nang sabay ang approach na parang assignment matrix at ang twirling mitigation approach (kahit hindi eksaktong pareho ang twirling approach na ito sa TREX). Paulit-ulit itong tema sa pagsasama ng mitigation at suppression techniques: pumapalya ang bulag na pagdaragdag ng mga technique; kapag maingat silang pinagsama para \"aware\" sila sa isa't isa, gumagana ito at puwedeng maging malaking pakinabang.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "TREX only guarantees expectation values that are free of bias due to readout errors; it does not reduce variance, nor does it protect against other types of bias. It certainly does not correct all errors. However, it is often a substantial improvement, and it is achievable with much lower overhead than some other methods that require learning full noise models. TREX is often the lowest-cost and most robust readout mitigation available, making it an excellent default in experiments.\n"
msgstr "Ang ginagarantiya lang ng TREX ay mga expectation value na walang bias dahil sa readout errors; hindi nito binabawasan ang variance, at hindi rin nito pinoprotektahan laban sa ibang uri ng bias. Tiyak na hindi nito naitatama ang lahat ng error. Gayunpaman, madalas itong malaking pagbuti, at magagawa ito sa mas mababang overhead kaysa sa ilang ibang method na kailangang matutunan ang buong noise models. Madalas ang TREX ang pinakamura at pinakamatatag na readout mitigation na magagamit, kaya mahusay itong default sa mga eksperimento.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "TREX with Qiskit {#trex-with-qiskit}"
msgstr "TREX gamit ang Qiskit {#trex-with-qiskit}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here we create a simple two-qubit circuit, and use Estimator to estimate the expectation value of a very simple observable, `ZX`. The ideal expectation value is 1. We will compare the expectation values with and without TREX.\n"
msgstr "Dito gagawa tayo ng simpleng two-qubit circuit, at gagamitin ang Estimator para tantiyahin ang expectation value ng isang napakasimpleng observable, `ZX`. Ang ideal na expectation value ay 1. Ikukumpara natin ang mga expectation value na may TREX at wala.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Create the simple quantum circuit.\n"
msgstr "Gawin ang simpleng quantum circuit.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/46c98dd4-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/46c98dd4-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Transpile the circuit using a pass manager.\n"
msgstr "I-transpile ang circuit gamit ang pass manager.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Apply the same layout from transpilation to the observable.\n"
msgstr "Ilapat sa observable ang parehong layout mula sa transpilation.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Run the experiment on a real quantum computer. First we proceed with `reslience_level = 1`; this is the default, but we have made it explicit for distinguishing the two cases.\n"
msgstr "Patakbuhin ang eksperimento sa totoong quantum computer. Una, gagamitin natin ang `reslience_level = 1`; ito ang default, pero ginawa nating tahasan para maiba ang dalawang kaso.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we set the `resilience_level` to 0 to run without TREX.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon itinatakda natin ang `resilience_level` sa 0 para tumakbo nang walang TREX.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The expectation value is substantially closer to the ideal (1) with TREX, as expected.\n"
"Of course, this was a single expectation value on a single qubit. We now prepare a circuit using non-Clifford gates and many qubits to test whether the TREX improvement remains robust in a different setting.\n"
msgstr ""
"Mas malapit nang malaki ang expectation value sa ideal (1) kapag may TREX, gaya ng inaasahan.\n"
"Siyempre, iisang expectation value lang ito sa iisang qubit. Maghahanda tayo ngayon ng circuit na gumagamit ng non-Clifford gates at maraming qubit para subukin kung nananatiling matatag ang pagbuti ng TREX sa ibang setting.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here, there is nothing special about the rotation angle $7\\pi/8$. We simply chose any unequal superposition of $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$ in order to probe effects of asymmetric erroneous transitions and how they are treated by TREX. Given this state, the ideal expectation value of Z on every qubit, in the absence of any errors (readout or otherwise) would be:\n"
msgstr "Dito, walang espesyal sa rotation angle na $7\\pi/8$. Pumili lang tayo ng kahit anong hindi pantay na superposition ng $|0\\rangle$ at $|1\\rangle$ para masuri ang epekto ng asymmetric na maling transitions at kung paano ito hinahawakan ng TREX. Sa state na ito, ang ideal na expectation value ng Z sa bawat qubit, kung walang anumang error (readout man o iba pa), ay:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle = \\langle \\psi |Z|\\psi \\rangle = -0.9239\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle = \\langle \\psi |Z|\\psi \\rangle = -0.9239\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We therefore hope to obtain expectation values close to -0.924 on all qubits. We will see if TREX improves each expectation value individually and also whether it improves the average over all qubits.\n"
msgstr "Kaya umaasa tayong makakuha ng mga expectation value na malapit sa -0.924 sa lahat ng qubit. Titingnan natin kung pinapabuti ng TREX ang bawat expectation value nang isa-isa at kung pinapabuti rin nito ang average sa lahat ng qubit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-1}"
msgstr "Subukin ang iyong pang-unawa {#check-your-understanding-1}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Verify the expectation value above through direct calculation.\n"
msgstr "I-verify ang expectation value sa itaas sa pamamagitan ng direktang kalkulasyon.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = \\langle \\psi |Z|\\psi \\rangle\\\\\n"
"& = \\langle 0 |RX^\\dagger (7\\pi/8) \\quad Z \\quad RX(7\\pi/8)|0 \\rangle\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & \\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & \\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & \\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)-\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -2\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& = \\cos^2\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)-\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\\\\n"
"& = -0.9239...\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = \\langle \\psi |Z|\\psi \\rangle\\\\\n"
"& = \\langle 0 |RX^\\dagger (7\\pi/8) \\quad Z \\quad RX(7\\pi/8)|0 \\rangle\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & \\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & \\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) & \\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -i\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)-\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right) \\\\ -2\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& = \\cos^2\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)-\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{16}\\right)\\\\\n"
"& = -0.9239...\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We transpile using a pass manager.\n"
msgstr "Nagta-transpile tayo gamit ang pass manager.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we construct one observable per qubit so that we can estimate all twenty $\\langle Z_i\\rangle$ expectation values in a single workflow. That is, for the $i^\\text{th}$ qubit, we want an observable with all identities $I$, except at the $i^\\text{th}$ position, where we want a $Z$. Remember to apply your transpiled layout to your observables as well.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon gagawa tayo ng isang observable kada qubit para matantiya natin ang lahat ng dalawampung $\\langle Z_i\\rangle$ expectation value sa iisang workflow. Ibig sabihin, para sa $i^\\text{th}$ na qubit, gusto natin ng observable na puro identity $I$, maliban sa $i^\\text{th}$ na posisyon, kung saan gusto natin ng $Z$. Tandaang ilapat din ang transpiled layout mo sa mga observable mo.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we can execute. The code cell below uses [session mode](/guides/run-jobs-session); if you do not have access to sessions, you can replace it with [batch mode](/guides/run-jobs-batch) to run the same workflow but with possible queue times between circuits.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon puwede na tayong mag-execute. Gumagamit ang code cell sa ibaba ng [session mode](/guides/run-jobs-session); kung wala kang access sa sessions, puwede mo itong palitan ng [batch mode](/guides/run-jobs-batch) para patakbuhin ang parehong workflow pero may posibleng queue time sa pagitan ng mga circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here we compare the mean absolute errors with and without TREX.\n"
msgstr "Dito ikinukumpara natin ang mean absolute errors na may TREX at wala.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/03aff38d-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/03aff38d-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "TREX certainly appears to have helped. The expectation value on every qubit has improved, with the sole exception of qubit 16. Such an outlier is to be expected. We can quantify the overall application of TREX to the circuit by taking the average values.\n"
msgstr "Mukhang talagang nakatulong ang TREX. Bumuti ang expectation value sa bawat qubit, maliban sa qubit 16. Inaasahan ang ganoong outlier. Mabibilang natin ang kabuuang epekto ng TREX sa circuit sa pamamagitan ng pagkuha ng mga average value.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "On average, TREX moved our results about 2.9% closer to the ideal results, and left us with a result that is, on average, different from the theoretical ideal by just 0.4%. This is great, and this low-overhead improvement is exactly why TREX is the default error mitigation on Estimator. But we must once more emphasize that TREX is removing a bias in readout error, not correcting all our errors. We chose an initial state that had a good chance of being affected by asymmetry in readout. The effect would have been different if we had prepared each qubit in a different state. For a trivial argument why this must be the case, consider what would happen if we left each qubit in the ground state $|0\\rangle$. There would be no opportunity to observe (or correct) an asymmetry in readout error between the states $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr "Sa karaniwan, inilapit ng TREX ang mga resulta natin nang mga 2.9% sa ideal na resulta, at iniwan tayo ng resultang, sa karaniwan, 0.4% lang ang pagkakaiba sa teoretikal na ideal. Magaling ito, at ito mismo ang dahilan kung bakit ang mababang-overhead na pagbuting ito ang default na error mitigation sa Estimator. Ngunit dapat nating idiin muli na inaalis ng TREX ang bias sa readout error, hindi itinatama ang lahat ng error natin. Pumili tayo ng initial state na malaki ang tsansang maapektuhan ng asymmetry sa readout. Iba sana ang epekto kung inihanda natin ang bawat qubit sa ibang state. Para sa simpleng argumento kung bakit kailangang ganoon, isipin kung ano ang mangyayari kung iniwan natin ang bawat qubit sa ground state na $|0\\rangle$. Walang pagkakataong makita (o maitama) ang asymmetry sa readout error sa pagitan ng mga state na $|0\\rangle$ at $|1\\rangle$.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) {#zero-noise-extrapolation-zne}"
msgstr "Zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) {#zero-noise-extrapolation-zne}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) is one of the most widely used error-mitigation techniques. The general strategy is also easy to understand, though the details of its implementation are complex. ZNE works by:\n"
msgstr "Ang Zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) ay isa sa pinakamalawakang ginagamit na error-mitigation techniques. Madali ring maunawaan ang pangkalahatang estratehiya, kahit masalimuot ang mga detalye ng implementasyon. Ganito gumagana ang ZNE:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Running a target circuit at multiple \"noise levels\" &mdash; modifications to the circuit that cause its noise to scale in predictable ways\n"
msgstr "Pagpapatakbo ng target circuit sa maraming \"noise levels\" &mdash; mga pagbabago sa circuit na nagpapalaki sa noise nito sa mga predictable na paraan\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Estimating the observable of interest for each noise scale.\n"
msgstr "Pagtantiya sa observable na pinagtutuunan para sa bawat noise scale.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Extrapolating back to zero noise using a fitting model (linear, polynomial, exponential, and so on).\n"
msgstr "Pag-extrapolate pabalik sa zero noise gamit ang fitting model (linear, polynomial, exponential, at iba pa).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The result is an estimate of what the observable would have been in the zero-noise limit implied by the extrapolation model.\n"
msgstr "Ang resulta ay tantiya kung ano sana ang observable sa zero-noise limit na ipinahihiwatig ng extrapolation model.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A diagram showing many expectation values at many noise levels. These results lie approximately on a line, and the best fit line is traced backwards to the zero-noise case. This is zero noise extrapolation.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/zne.avif)\n"
msgstr "![A diagram showing many expectation values at many noise levels. These results lie approximately on a line, and the best fit line is traced backwards to the zero-noise case. This is zero noise extrapolation.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/zne.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "ZNE does not guarantee that noise increases smoothly or linearly as we modify circuits. Instead, it **assumes** that noise scaling is sufficiently well-behaved to allow extrapolation. The complexity in ZNE is in how one modifies circuits to satisfy this assumption as well as possible. We will examine some approaches in this lesson.\n"
msgstr "Hindi ginagarantiya ng ZNE na tataas nang maayos o linear ang noise habang binabago natin ang mga circuit. Sa halip, **ipinapalagay** nito na sapat na maayos ang pagkilos ng noise scaling para payagan ang extrapolation. Ang pagiging masalimuot ng ZNE ay nasa kung paano babaguhin ang mga circuit para matugunan hangga't maaari ang assumption na ito. Susuriin natin ang ilang approach sa lesson na ito.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "ZNE resource scaling {#zne-resource-scaling}"
msgstr "ZNE resource scaling {#zne-resource-scaling}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In ZNE, the overhead is mostly quantum overhead. That is, to obtain expectation values at $k$ noise levels, you have to carry out your experiment on $k$ circuits, each with many shots. To maintain a fixed statistical precision, the total number of samples generally increases as additional noise-scaled circuits are introduced.\n"
msgstr "Sa ZNE, ang overhead ay karamihan quantum overhead. Ibig sabihin, para makuha ang mga expectation value sa $k$ noise levels, kailangan mong isagawa ang eksperimento mo sa $k$ circuit, bawat isa ay maraming shots. Para mapanatili ang fixed na statistical precision, karaniwang tumataas ang kabuuang bilang ng samples habang dumarami ang mga noise-scaled circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The number of noise levels you examine, $k$, will depend on the method used to scale the noise. For one simple noise-scaling technique (called \"gate-folding\", discussed below), $k$ is commonly between 3 and 5. A different method (probabilistic noise amplification (PNA)) allows for non-integer noise factors, and typically results in lower noise levels than gate-folding. The total overhead scales roughly as:\n"
msgstr "Ang bilang ng noise levels na susuriin mo, $k$, ay nakadepende sa method na ginamit sa pag-scale ng noise. Para sa isang simpleng noise-scaling technique (tinatawag na \"gate-folding\", tatalakayin sa ibaba), karaniwang nasa pagitan ng 3 at 5 ang $k$. Ang ibang method (probabilistic noise amplification (PNA)) ay nagpapahintulot ng non-integer na noise factors, at karaniwang nagreresulta sa mas mababang noise levels kaysa sa gate-folding. Ang kabuuang overhead ay lumalaki nang humigit-kumulang ayon sa:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\text{Runtime} \\sim k \\cdot S\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\text{Runtime} \\sim k \\cdot S\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where $S$ is the baseline shot count. Another thing to keep in mind is that if noise scaling involves repeated gates, the circuit depth might increase by a factor proportional to the noise-scaling value. The scaling of noise that comes with increased depth is the point, but keep the total depth in mind.\n"
msgstr "kung saan ang $S$ ay ang baseline na bilang ng shots. Isa pang dapat tandaan: kung may paulit-ulit na gates ang noise scaling, maaaring tumaas ang circuit depth nang may factor na proporsyonal sa noise-scaling value. Ang pag-scale ng noise na kasabay ng pagtaas ng depth ang mismong layunin, pero bantayan ang kabuuang depth.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "ZNE is well-suited to problems in which the following points apply:\n"
msgstr "Angkop ang ZNE sa mga problemang kung saan totoo ang mga sumusunod na punto:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "You need a general-purpose mitigation method effective on many error types.\n"
msgstr "Kailangan mo ng general-purpose na mitigation method na epektibo sa maraming uri ng error.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Circuit depth is not too large; ZNE fails when the scaled circuit becomes too noisy to extrapolate.\n"
msgstr "Hindi masyadong malaki ang circuit depth; pumapalya ang ZNE kapag naging sobrang noisy na ang scaled circuit para i-extrapolate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "You can tolerate moderate runtime overhead for improved accuracy.\n"
msgstr "Kaya mong tanggapin ang katamtamang runtime overhead kapalit ng mas mataas na accuracy.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In practice this makes ZNE ideal for many variational circuits, expectation-value estimation, and other near-term quantum algorithms. It is implemented in Estimator using `resilience_level = 2`. Note that ZNE extrapolates in a space that has a single outcome as a function of noise throughout the circuit. That means it is amenable to estimation problems, but not to sampling problems. It is not designed to work on distributions of bitstrings.\n"
msgstr "Sa praktika, dahil dito ideal ang ZNE para sa maraming variational circuit, expectation-value estimation, at iba pang near-term quantum algorithm. Naipapatupad ito sa Estimator gamit ang `resilience_level = 2`. Tandaan na nag-e-extrapolate ang ZNE sa isang space na may iisang outcome bilang function ng noise sa buong circuit. Ibig sabihin, angkop ito sa mga estimation problem, pero hindi sa mga sampling problem. Hindi ito idinisenyo para gumana sa mga distribution ng bitstrings.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Noise factors {#noise-factors}"
msgstr "Noise factors {#noise-factors}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "How exactly can we increase the level of noise in a circuit? There are two main ways, although many variants exist.\n"
msgstr "Paano nga ba natin eksaktong maitataas ang antas ng noise sa isang circuit? May dalawang pangunahing paraan, bagaman maraming variant.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Replace a gate or block of gates $G$ with $GG^\\dagger G$. Because gates on quantum computers are unitary, we know that $G^\\dagger = G^{-1}$ so that logically: $GG^\\dagger G = G$. However, every repetition of $G$ or $G^\\dagger$ adds similar or identical error, allowing us to scale up noise/error levels. Note that the replacement shown above takes us from the original noise of gate $G$ (noise level 1) to three times that noise level in $GG^\\dagger G$ (noise level 3). In practice, we can repeat the last two gates $\\ell$ times: $G(G^\\dagger G)^\\ell$ to obtain a noise level $2\\ell+1$. This is called **gate folding**, and it is a very common noise scaling technique.\n"
msgstr "Palitan ang isang gate o block ng gates na $G$ ng $GG^\\dagger G$. Dahil unitary ang mga gate sa quantum computers, alam nating $G^\\dagger = G^{-1}$ kaya lohikal na: $GG^\\dagger G = G$. Gayunpaman, bawat pag-uulit ng $G$ o $G^\\dagger$ ay nagdaragdag ng magkatulad o parehong error, kaya napapataas natin ang noise/error levels. Tandaan na ang pagpapalit sa itaas ay nagdadala sa atin mula sa orihinal na noise ng gate $G$ (noise level 1) tungo sa tatlong beses ng noise level na iyon sa $GG^\\dagger G$ (noise level 3). Sa praktika, maaari nating ulitin ang huling dalawang gate nang $\\ell$ beses: $G(G^\\dagger G)^\\ell$ para makakuha ng noise level na $2\\ell+1$. Tinatawag itong **gate folding**, at napakakaraniwang noise scaling technique ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are other methods for increasing the noise level, such as probabilistic error amplification (PEA). In PEA, we first learn a suitable noise model for the circuit, typically expressed as a Pauli channel. Noise is then amplified by sampling from a rescaled version of this learned noise channel. By adjusting the sampling, you can continuously scale the effective noise strength, including to non-integer values.\n"
msgstr "May iba pang method para itaas ang noise level, tulad ng probabilistic error amplification (PEA). Sa PEA, una nating pinag-aaralan ang angkop na noise model para sa circuit, karaniwang ipinapahayag bilang Pauli channel. Pagkatapos ay pinalalakas ang noise sa pamamagitan ng pag-sample mula sa rescaled na bersyon ng natutunang noise channel na ito. Sa pag-aayos ng sampling, tuloy-tuloy mong mai-scale ang epektibong lakas ng noise, kasama ang mga non-integer na halaga.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In practice, gate folding can very reliably scale errors, but it can also lead to very deep circuits. If you only need three noise levels (1, 3, and 5), it means increasing your circuit depth by a factor of 5 and still obtaining reasonable results. Thus, if your target circuit has a transpiled, two-qubit depth of 60 or more, PEA is likely a better fit than gate-folding.\n"
msgstr "Sa praktika, napakapagkakatiwalaan ng gate folding sa pag-scale ng mga error, pero maaari rin itong humantong sa napakalalim na circuit. Kung tatlong noise levels lang ang kailangan mo (1, 3, at 5), ibig sabihin nito ay tataasan mo ang circuit depth nang 5 beses at makakakuha pa rin ng makatwirang resulta. Kaya, kung ang target circuit mo ay may transpiled na two-qubit depth na 60 o higit pa, malamang na mas bagay ang PEA kaysa sa gate-folding.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Gates can be folded either locally (meaning you repeat $GG^\\dagger G$ for each two-qubit gate as soon as it occurs), or they can be grouped into a block of gates and the block can be repeated (like gates $ABC\\rightarrow ABC(ABC)^\\dagger ABC$). Often local folding is preferable for assumptions made by ZNE, but both are possible.\n"
msgstr "Maaaring i-fold ang mga gate nang lokal (ibig sabihin inuulit mo ang $GG^\\dagger G$ para sa bawat two-qubit gate sa sandaling mangyari ito), o maaari silang igrupo sa isang block ng gates at inuulit ang block (tulad ng mga gate na $ABC\\rightarrow ABC(ABC)^\\dagger ABC$). Madalas na mas gusto ang local folding para sa mga assumption ng ZNE, pero posible ang pareho.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A lot of nuance goes into the statistical treatment of noise-scaled results in ZNE. For example, at five times the noise level your results are certain to have higher variance and be less reliable. Should that be treated with the same weight as lower-noise data points? No. However, for the purposes of seeing ZNE in action, we will ignore some of this complexity for a toy model experiment, in which we will build folded circuits by hand, and do a naive extrapolation with equal weightings to illustrate the key steps. But we will follow this with an experiment using the ZNE functionality built into Qiskit, and we will see that it does a substantially better job of handling the complexity we are skipping.\n"
msgstr "Maraming nuance ang pagtrato sa istatistika ng mga noise-scaled na resulta sa ZNE. Halimbawa, sa limang beses ng noise level, tiyak na mas mataas ang variance ng mga resulta mo at mas hindi mapagkakatiwalaan. Dapat ba itong pantay na timbangin sa mga data point na mas mababa ang noise? Hindi. Gayunpaman, para makita ang ZNE sa aksyon, babalewalain natin ang ilan sa komplikasyong ito para sa isang toy model na eksperimento, kung saan bubuo tayo ng mga folded circuit nang mano-mano, at gagawa ng naive na extrapolation na may pantay na timbang para ilarawan ang mga pangunahing hakbang. Pero susundan natin ito ng eksperimentong gumagamit ng ZNE functionality na built-in sa Qiskit, at makikita nating mas mahusay nitong napangangasiwaan ang komplikasyong nilaktawan natin.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "ZNE by hand {#zne-by-hand}"
msgstr "ZNE nang mano-mano {#zne-by-hand}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We start by selecting a quantum circuit and an observable for which we can calculate the ideal expectation value in the absence of noise and errors. We choose a GHZ state $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(|0\\rangle^{\\otimes N}+|1\\rangle^{\\otimes N}\\right)$, with an even number of qubits. Our observable is $Z^{\\otimes N}$. We choose this for a number of reasons:\n"
"* The expectation value $\\langle Z^{\\otimes N}\\rangle = 1$ is trivial to calculate in the ideal case. Because the number of qubits is even, we either get a `1` from every $Z$, multiplied together, or we get a `-1` from every $Z$, multiplied together an even number of times.\n"
"* This will be fairly sensitive to errors and noise, since a single error on one qubit can flip the overall outcome from that shot from +1 to -1.\n"
"* The expectation value is far from 0. ZNE can encounter difficulties when the ideal expectation value is very near zero, because high noise levels will tend to randomize outcomes, making them positive as often as negative. The result is that high noise values will end up yielding values close to the ideal; this can make extrapolation very tricky.\n"
msgstr ""
"Magsisimula tayo sa pagpili ng quantum circuit at observable kung saan makakalkula natin ang ideal na expectation value kapag walang noise at error. Pinipili natin ang GHZ state na $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(|0\\rangle^{\\otimes N}+|1\\rangle^{\\otimes N}\\right)$, na may even na bilang ng qubit. Ang observable natin ay $Z^{\\otimes N}$. Pinipili natin ito dahil sa ilang dahilan:\n"
"* Napakadaling kalkulahin ang expectation value na $\\langle Z^{\\otimes N}\\rangle = 1$ sa ideal na kaso. Dahil even ang bilang ng qubit, makakakuha tayo ng `1` mula sa bawat $Z$, pinarami nang magkakasama, o `-1` mula sa bawat $Z$, pinarami nang magkakasama nang even na bilang ng beses.\n"
"* Medyo sensitibo ito sa error at noise, dahil ang iisang error sa isang qubit ay kayang baligtarin ang kabuuang outcome ng shot na iyon mula +1 tungong -1.\n"
"* Malayo sa 0 ang expectation value. Maaaring magkaproblema ang ZNE kapag napakalapit sa zero ng ideal na expectation value, dahil ang mataas na noise levels ay may tendensiyang i-randomize ang mga outcome, kaya kasing-dalas na positibo ang mga ito gaya ng negatibo. Ang resulta, ang mataas na noise values ay magbibigay ng mga halagang malapit sa ideal; maaari nitong gawing napakatricky ang extrapolation.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "With this motivation in mind, let's build our circuits.\n"
msgstr "Sa motibasyong ito, buuin na natin ang mga circuit natin.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note the subtlety that here $\\text{CNOT}^{-1} = \\text{CNOT}^\\dagger = \\text{CNOT}$. Even though gate folding was advertised as consisting of operators of the form $GG^\\dagger G$, here that same structure looks like CNOT CNOT CNOT.\n"
msgstr "Pansinin ang subtlety na dito $\\text{CNOT}^{-1} = \\text{CNOT}^\\dagger = \\text{CNOT}$. Kahit na ang gate folding ay inilarawan bilang binubuo ng mga operator na may anyong $GG^\\dagger G$, dito ang parehong istrukturang iyon ay mukhang CNOT CNOT CNOT.\n"

