msgid "The effective inverse noise map is realized statistically in classical post-processing. This allows for more general inversion of noise channels that would otherwise have required non-physical steps like negative probabilities.\n"
msgstr "Ang epektibong inverse noise map ay natutupad nang istatistikal sa classical post-processing. Pinapayagan nito ang mas pangkalahatang inversion ng mga noise channel na kung hindi ay mangangailangan ng mga hindi pisikal na hakbang tulad ng negatibong probabilidad.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The price we pay for this is variance.\n"
"Each shot formerly had a \"magnitude\" equal to the possible measurement outcome magnitudes $|\\pm 1| = 1$. After scaling by the weight factor $W$, it has magnitude $W$ (1.375 in our example above).\n"
"$$\n"
"\\text{variance} \\propto W^2 \\approx 1.89\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang kapalit nito ay variance.\n"
"Dati, ang bawat shot ay may \"magnitude\" na katumbas ng mga posibleng magnitude ng resulta ng measurement na $|\\pm 1| = 1$. Pagkatapos i-scale gamit ang weight factor na $W$, ang magnitude nito ay $W$ (1.375 sa halimbawa natin sa itaas).\n"
"$$\n"
"\\text{variance} \\propto W^2 \\approx 1.89\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "That is the origin of the sampling overhead of PEC.\n"
msgstr "Iyan ang pinagmulan ng sampling overhead ng PEC.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Built-in PEC {#built-in-pec}"
msgstr "Built-in na PEC {#built-in-pec}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we will use PEC as it is built into IBM Quantum primitives. It is critical here to distinguish between full gate-level PEC and a local noise learning model for more scalable PEC, which is used by IBM Quantum primitives. The overhead of using full PEC would would restrict our application of it to small circuits. That's fine, but it is more useful to have a tool that can scale to a size at which quantum computing becomes valuable for real-world problems. For this reason, IBM Quantum primtives use a layer-wise PEC using a learned Pauli-Lindblad noise model, with aggressive scalability safeguards. In this implementation of PEC, noise is learned per repeated circuit layer, not per gate instance, and the noise model is Pauli-twirled, local, and Markovian. Clifford gates are absorbed into the noise model, while non-Clifford gates dominate the mitigation cost. Sampling overhead is capped to avoid exponential blow-ups.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon, gagamitin natin ang PEC na built-in sa mga IBM Quantum primitive. Mahalagang makilala rito ang pagkakaiba ng buong gate-level na PEC at ng local noise learning model para sa mas scalable na PEC, na siyang ginagamit ng mga IBM Quantum primitive. Ang overhead ng paggamit ng buong PEC ay maglilimita sa paggamit nito sa maliliit na circuit. Okay lang iyon, pero mas kapaki-pakinabang ang isang tool na kayang mag-scale sa laki kung saan nagiging mahalaga ang quantum computing para sa mga totoong problema. Dahil dito, gumagamit ang mga IBM Quantum primitive ng layer-wise na PEC gamit ang natutunang Pauli-Lindblad noise model, na may agresibong mga safeguard para sa scalability. Sa implementasyong ito ng PEC, natututunan ang noise bawat paulit-ulit na circuit layer, hindi bawat gate instance, at ang noise model ay Pauli-twirled, local, at Markovian. Ang mga Clifford gate ay sinasama sa noise model, habang ang mga non-Clifford gate ang nangingibabaw sa gastos ng mitigation. May limitasyon ang sampling overhead upang maiwasan ang exponential na pagsabog.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will apply the PEC built in to IBM Quantum primitives at two scales: small (six qubits) and mid-sized (30 qubits) to see how cost is kept management in this version of PEC. Within each size experiment, we will also explicitly compare the results from PEC to those with no mitigation, with ZNE, and to the ideal value.\n"
msgstr "Ia-apply natin ang PEC na built-in sa mga IBM Quantum primitive sa dalawang sukat: maliit (anim na qubit) at katamtaman (30 qubit) para makita kung paano napapanatiling napapamahalaan ang gastos sa bersyong ito ng PEC. Sa bawat sukat ng eksperimento, tahasan din nating ihahambing ang mga resulta ng PEC sa mga walang mitigation, sa ZNE, at sa ideal na halaga.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We start by importing a few necessary packages.\n"
msgstr "Magsisimula tayo sa pag-import ng ilang kinakailangang package.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We choose an observable to estimate. The observable below is the Hamiltonian of a chain of Ising-like spins with nearest-neighbor coupling only in the $X$ component of their interaction, and a single-qubit term in $Z$, as though due to an external field in $Z$.\n"
msgstr "Pumipili tayo ng observable na ie-estimate. Ang observable sa ibaba ay ang Hamiltonian ng isang chain ng mga Ising-like na spin na may nearest-neighbor coupling lamang sa $X$ component ng kanilang interaksyon, at isang single-qubit term sa $Z$, na para bang dahil sa external field sa $Z$.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathcal{H} = J_X\\sum_i{X_iX_{i+1}}+B\\sum_i{Z_i}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathcal{H} = J_X\\sum_i{X_iX_{i+1}}+B\\sum_i{Z_i}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "There is no special reason for the selection of this Hamiltonian. We simply need a combination of an observable and circuit that is non-trivial so that error mitigation can do its work. We explicitly scale the Hamiltonian (using the coefficients), dividing out the number of qubits so that the energy will remain at the same order of magnitude as we increase the number of qubits. We begin with only six.\n"
msgstr "Walang espesyal na dahilan sa pagpili ng Hamiltonian na ito. Kailangan lang natin ng kombinasyon ng observable at circuit na hindi trivial para magawa ng error mitigation ang trabaho nito. Tahasan nating i-scale ang Hamiltonian (gamit ang mga coefficient), at hinahati natin sa bilang ng mga qubit para manatili ang energy sa parehong order of magnitude habang dinadagdagan natin ang bilang ng mga qubit. Magsisimula tayo sa anim lamang.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As in the choice of Hamiltonian, the circuit for state preparation below is not chosen for any physical reasons. Rather, it is chosen to have sufficient two-qubit depth and sufficiently many non-Clifford gates that PEC can be useful. There is no reason to think that this circuit is a good approximation of the ground state of this chain of spins, for example.\n"
msgstr "Tulad ng pagpili ng Hamiltonian, ang circuit para sa state preparation sa ibaba ay hindi pinili dahil sa anumang pisikal na dahilan. Sa halip, pinili ito para magkaroon ng sapat na two-qubit depth at sapat na dami ng mga non-Clifford gate upang maging kapaki-pakinabang ang PEC. Walang dahilan para isiping mahusay na approximation ng ground state ng chain ng mga spin na ito ang circuit na ito, halimbawa.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d61a4174-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d61a4174-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We transpile our circuit and apply the resulting layout to our observables.\n"
msgstr "Ta-transpile natin ang circuit natin at ia-apply ang resultang layout sa mga observable natin.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We obtain the ideal expectation value using the Statevector simulator.\n"
msgstr "Kinukuha natin ang ideal na expectation value gamit ang Statevector simulator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We expect that other ideal values will be similar in size. Now we run this on a real IBM quantum computer with no error mitigation.\n"
msgstr "Inaasahan nating magkakasinlaki ang iba pang ideal na halaga. Ngayon, patatakbuhin natin ito sa totoong IBM quantum computer nang walang error mitigation.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we apply ZNE to the same problem.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon, ia-apply natin ang ZNE sa parehong problema.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we apply PEC.\n"
msgstr "Panghuli, ia-apply natin ang PEC.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Below we plot our results and compare.\n"
msgstr "Sa ibaba, ipi-plot natin ang mga resulta at ihahambing.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/626e82d3-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/626e82d3-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This worked very well. Our estimate using PEC is just 0.2% from the ideal value.\n"
msgstr "Napakahusay ng kinalabasan nito. Ang estimate natin gamit ang PEC ay 0.2% lang ang layo sa ideal na halaga.\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "But remember how the quantum overhead grows exponentially. We enforced a maximum overhead of `max_overhead = 100`. From the time and the quality of the results, it looks like we might not have hit that maximum. However, if we scale up our calculation and leave that maximum in place, we will hit it, and our results will suffer.\n"
msgstr "Pero tandaan kung paanong exponential ang paglaki ng quantum overhead. Nagtakda tayo ng maximum na overhead na `max_overhead = 100`. Mula sa oras at sa kalidad ng mga resulta, mukhang maaaring hindi natin naabot ang maximum na iyon. Gayunpaman, kung palalakihin natin ang kalkulasyon at iiwan ang maximum na iyon, maaabot natin ito, at maaapektuhan ang mga resulta natin.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can see what happens when we scale our circuit up to 30 qubits. Interestingly, on many laptops the Statevector simulator will start to time out around 24-28 qubits. We could find computational ways around this. But instead, let's embrace the fact that quantum computers will be able to solve some problems at a scale at which classical computers cannot keep up. That is the point. What can we do, then, to validate our quantum computing results in those cases? There are several potential answers:\n"
"* Some problems are difficult to solve but easy to check (like prime factoring as in Shor's algorithm).\n"
"* Some problems have parameter regimes or flavors of the problem that reduce in complexity and can be solved classically, or even analytically, to compare a few points.\n"
"In this case, we chose a Hamiltonian that is intrinsic (we divided out the number of qubits). The ideal expectation value should be fairly stable. Let's see how the ideal expectation value varies with the number of qubits and see if we can make classical predictions about the value at 30 qubits.\n"
msgstr ""
"Makikita natin ang mangyayari kapag pinalaki natin ang circuit hanggang 30 qubit. Kapansin-pansin, sa maraming laptop ay magsisimulang mag-time out ang Statevector simulator sa paligid ng 24-28 qubit. Makakahanap tayo ng mga computational na paraan para malusutan ito. Pero sa halip, yakapin natin ang katotohanang makakalutas ang mga quantum computer ng ilang problema sa sukat na hindi kayang sabayan ng mga classical computer. Iyon ang punto. Ano ang magagawa natin, kung gayon, para i-validate ang mga resulta ng quantum computing natin sa mga ganoong kaso? Maraming posibleng sagot:\n"
"* Ang ilang problema ay mahirap lutasin pero madaling i-check (tulad ng prime factoring sa algorithm ni Shor).\n"
"* Ang ilang problema ay may mga parameter regime o bersyon na bumababa ang complexity at maaaring lutasin nang classical, o kahit analytical, para maihambing ang ilang punto.\n"
"Sa kasong ito, pumili tayo ng Hamiltonian na intrinsic (hinati natin sa bilang ng mga qubit). Dapat medyo matatag ang ideal na expectation value. Tingnan natin kung paano nag-iiba ang ideal na expectation value ayon sa bilang ng mga qubit at kung makakagawa tayo ng mga classical na prediksyon tungkol sa halaga sa 30 qubit.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/e5d5f7f5-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/e5d5f7f5-1.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indeed, we see that we have selected a Hamiltonian and state that does not change with the number of qubits. This is a very poor use for a research application of quantum computing, but an excellent one for benchmarking error mitigation techniques. We can confidently use this fixed expectation value as the ideal result, even as we scale up the number of qubits for the testing of error mitigation.\n"
msgstr "Tunay ngang nakikita nating pumili tayo ng Hamiltonian at state na hindi nagbabago kasabay ng bilang ng mga qubit. Napakahina nitong gamitin para sa research application ng quantum computing, pero napakahusay para sa pag-benchmark ng mga error mitigation technique. Makakapagtiwala tayong gamitin ang nakapirming expectation value na ito bilang ideal na resulta, kahit na pinalalaki natin ang bilang ng mga qubit para sa pagsubok ng error mitigation.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we run this larger circuit with ZNE.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon, patatakbuhin natin ang mas malaking circuit na ito gamit ang ZNE.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we run the 30-qubit example with PEC.\n"
msgstr "Panghuli, patatakbuhin natin ang 30-qubit na halimbawa gamit ang PEC.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d4e98091-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d4e98091-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Not only did PEC take much longer than the other options, but we clearly reached our maximum overhead threshold. The PEC results paid the overhead price, but the overhead cap prevented sufficiently accurate cancellation of the learned noise model. This is critical. At six qubits, we were able to leverage PEC and get better results with only a modest increase in quantum overhead (about a factor of 5). At 30 qubits, we paid a much higher price (about a factor of 60 times higher) and the results were even worse than the unmitigated estimate. This is not to say that PEC can never work with 30 qubits; that might be circuit-dependent. The message is simply that the exponential growth in quantum overhead makes it difficult to run for large circuits.\n"
msgstr "Hindi lang mas matagal ang PEC kaysa sa ibang opsyon, kundi malinaw ring naabot natin ang maximum na overhead threshold. Binayaran ng mga resulta ng PEC ang presyo ng overhead, pero pinigilan ng overhead cap ang sapat na tumpak na pagkansela ng natutunang noise model. Mahalaga ito. Sa anim na qubit, nagamit natin ang PEC at nakakuha ng mas magagandang resulta sa katamtamang pagtaas lang ng quantum overhead (humigit-kumulang 5 beses). Sa 30 qubit, mas mataas ang binayaran natin (humigit-kumulang 60 beses na mas mataas) at mas masama pa ang mga resulta kaysa sa unmitigated na estimate. Hindi ibig sabihin nito na hindi kailanman gagana ang PEC sa 30 qubit; maaaring nakadepende ito sa circuit. Ang mensahe ay simpleng nagpapahirap patakbuhin para sa malalaking circuit ang exponential na paglaki ng quantum overhead.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Key takeaways: {#key-takeaways}"
msgstr "Mga pangunahing takeaway: {#key-takeaways}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measurement mitigation often helps and usually carries low overhead: it removes a bias that does not scale with circuit depth and it composes cleanly with nearly everything.\n"
msgstr "Madalas nakakatulong ang measurement mitigation at kadalasang mababa ang overhead nito: inaalis nito ang bias na hindi sumasabay sa circuit depth at malinis itong nakakasama sa halos lahat.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some techniques cannot be combined. Even when they can be, make sure you combine them in a consistent way such that one is not undoing the other. A good example is ensuring that twirling is on during the calibration for M3 if those methods are to be combined.\n"
msgstr "Hindi maaaring pagsamahin ang ilang technique. Kahit kapag maaari, tiyaking pinagsasama ninyo ang mga ito sa pare-parehong paraan upang hindi kinakansela ng isa ang isa pa. Magandang halimbawa ang pagtiyak na naka-on ang twirling habang nasa calibration para sa M3 kung pagsasamahin ang mga pamamaraang iyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "PEC and ZNE should be thought of as alternatives to each other. They solve the same problem with opposite tradeoffs:\n"
msgstr "Dapat ituring ang PEC at ZNE bilang mga alternatibo sa isa't isa. Nilulutas nila ang parehong problema na may magkasalungat na tradeoff:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '  - '
#, no-wrap
msgid "PEC: yields unbiased expectation values at the cost of exponential variance.\n"
msgstr "PEC: nagbibigay ng mga unbiased na expectation value kapalit ng exponential na variance.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '  - '
#, no-wrap
msgid "ZNE generally yields residual bias that depends on the extrapolation model, but at moderate cost.\n"
msgstr "Karaniwang nagbibigay ang ZNE ng natitirang bias na nakadepende sa extrapolation model, pero sa katamtamang gastos.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "DD can help with long idle periods, but it reshapes the noise model and can invalidate extrapolation or inversion assumptions.\n"
msgstr "Makakatulong ang DD sa mahahabang idle period, pero binabago nito ang hugis ng noise model at maaari nitong mapawalang-bisa ang mga assumption ng extrapolation o inversion.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli twirling does not reduce noise. It reshapes it and often enables other techniques to work better (ZNE and PEC).\n"
msgstr "Hindi binabawasan ng Pauli twirling ang noise. Binabago nito ang hugis nito at madalas nitong napapagana nang mas maayos ang ibang technique (ZNE at PEC).\n"
